  m_Name: BasilWeatherRead
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: HiddenHarbours.World::HiddenHarbours.World.WeatherReadDef
  Id: weather_read.basil_samson
  ReadHour: 7
  OutlookHour: 15
  LookWindowHours: 2
  LookSamples: 5
  ModerateFrom: 2
  FreshFrom: 6
  TooMuchFrom: 8
  GaleFrom: 11
  ChangeAtLeast: 1.5
  CompassWords:
  - north
  - nor'east
  - east
  - sou'east
  - south
  - sou'west
  - west
  - nor'west
  LightLines:
  - 'Barely a breath this morning, and what there is comes off the {from}.'
  - 'Light air out of the {from}. Hardly a cat''s paw on her.'
  - 'Flat as a plate, near enough. A light air from the {from}.'
  ModerateLines:
  - 'Nice little breeze out of the {from} this morning.'
  - 'Wind''s {from}, a working breeze and no more.'
  - 'Steady breeze from the {from}, enough to put a ripple on her.'
  FreshLines:
  - 'Fresh out of the {from} this morning, and a bit of a chop with it.'
  - 'She''s freshening from the {from}. You''ll feel it on the oars.'
  - 'Wind''s {from} and fresh. White caps out past the point.'
  TooMuchLines:
  - 'Blowing hard out of the {from}, white water all along the point.'
  - 'Wind''s {from}, and too much of it. Hear it in the wires?'
  - 'She''s kicking up from the {from}. That''s a lop no dory wants.'
  GaleLines:
  - 'Blowing a gale from the {from}. I''d not stand on the end of the wharf, never mind row.'
  - 'A whole gale out of the {from}. Even the gulls have given up.'
  - 'Gale of wind from the {from}, and the sea''s gone white.'
  HoldingLines:
  - 'Holds like that all day, I''d say. Fair for the dory.'
  - 'Won''t change much before dark. A good day to row.'
  - 'She''ll keep like that. Take the dory out and enjoy it.'
  BuildingLines:
  - 'Comes up a bit by afternoon, but nothing a dory can''t take. Mind the row home.'
  - 'More of it later on. Still fit for rowing; keep your bow to it.'
  - 'Freshens after noon, but not past what a dory can stand.'
  EasingLines:
  - 'Eases off by afternoon. Fair for the dory, and fairer later.'
  - 'Dropping away later. A lovely row by the afternoon.'
  - 'Lies down after noon. You''ll have it easy coming home.'
  HomeEarlyLines:
  - 'But it builds by afternoon, too much for a dory then. Row early and be home by noon.'
  - 'Comes up hard later on. Go out early if you go, and don''t be late back.'
  - 'Won''t last. By afternoon it''s no day for a dory, so get your rowing done this morning.'
  StayInLines:
  - 'No day for a dory. Stay in the harbour.'
  - 'Leave the dory where she lies today. She''ll keep, and so will you.'
  - 'Not today. Mend your gear and let it blow.'
  StayInEasingLines:
  - 'Eases off later, but not enough to trust. Stay in the harbour today.'
  - 'She''ll lie down some by evening. Too late for the dory; leave it till tomorrow.'
  - 'Drops a bit after noon, but it''s still no day for a dory.'
